WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.493
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:05.573 --> 00:00:06.490
TIN-YUN HO: All right.

00:00:06.490 --> 00:00:08.170
My name is Tin-yun.

00:00:08.170 --> 00:00:11.038
And I'm a product manager
on the Cloud AI Team.

00:00:11.038 --> 00:00:12.580
And today, I'm here
to talk about how

00:00:12.580 --> 00:00:15.820
you can tackle some of your
high-value business problems

00:00:15.820 --> 00:00:19.030
using AutoML on one of the
most common types of data

00:00:19.030 --> 00:00:21.220
in the enterprise,
structured data.

00:00:24.010 --> 00:00:25.930
But before we dig in,
I wanted to first make

00:00:25.930 --> 00:00:27.430
sure everyone knows
what I'm talking

00:00:27.430 --> 00:00:30.820
about when I say machine
learning on structured data,

00:00:30.820 --> 00:00:31.510
right?

00:00:31.510 --> 00:00:34.930
So these use cases typically
start with a table of input,

00:00:34.930 --> 00:00:39.190
kind of like this, where each
row is an independent training

00:00:39.190 --> 00:00:40.570
example.

00:00:40.570 --> 00:00:44.800
And usually, one of these
columns is the target column.

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:47.290
And the remaining columns
are input features.

00:00:47.290 --> 00:00:50.200
And the goal is to create
a machine learning model

00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:53.470
to predict the value of the
target column accurately using

00:00:53.470 --> 00:00:57.170
the available input feature
values for that row.

00:00:57.170 --> 00:00:59.710
So for example, here you may
have a-- this is a [? toy ?]

00:00:59.710 --> 00:01:00.680
example--

00:01:00.680 --> 00:01:03.160
you might have a table
of historic offers

00:01:03.160 --> 00:01:04.470
from an online marketplace.

00:01:04.470 --> 00:01:06.970
And you want to create a model
that takes the available data

00:01:06.970 --> 00:01:10.180
on that offer to predict the
price at which that offer is

00:01:10.180 --> 00:01:11.293
sold.

00:01:11.293 --> 00:01:13.210
So for example, if you
want to provide pricing

00:01:13.210 --> 00:01:15.280
guidance to your sellers.

00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:17.890
And one common misconception
I wanted to point out

00:01:17.890 --> 00:01:20.590
is that people sometimes
think that you can only

00:01:20.590 --> 00:01:24.130
have simple values, like numbers
and classes, inside a table.

00:01:24.130 --> 00:01:26.170
But actually, especially
with modern data

00:01:26.170 --> 00:01:27.717
warehouse systems,
like BigQuery,

00:01:27.717 --> 00:01:29.800
you can put a rich set of
things in there-- things

00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:34.900
like timestamps, long pieces
of text, lists of features,

00:01:34.900 --> 00:01:36.940
potentially repeated
nested fields.

00:01:36.940 --> 00:01:41.310
And that would be
only getting started.

00:01:41.310 --> 00:01:44.340
So according to the
McKinsey Global Institute,

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:47.250
data in this basic
form, Structured Data,

00:01:47.250 --> 00:01:50.010
is likely to drive
most of AI's impact,

00:01:50.010 --> 00:01:53.410
with Time Series
being a close second.

00:01:53.410 --> 00:01:55.170
And this really
comes as no surprise.

00:01:55.170 --> 00:01:58.680
Because virtually every industry
has mission critical use cases

00:01:58.680 --> 00:02:01.750
that can be boiled down
into this tabular form.

00:02:01.750 --> 00:02:04.740
So for example, in retail,
predicting the likelihood

00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:07.180
of stock outs or
predicting price elasticity

00:02:07.180 --> 00:02:09.479
so you can optimize
your product inventory,

00:02:09.479 --> 00:02:12.678
in finance, predicting the risk
of large claims or defaults

00:02:12.678 --> 00:02:14.220
or predicting the
likelihood of fraud

00:02:14.220 --> 00:02:18.390
in this transaction in
order to manage your risk,

00:02:18.390 --> 00:02:20.683
in marketing, predicting
lifetime value,

00:02:20.683 --> 00:02:23.100
like how much they'll spend
on your site in the next three

00:02:23.100 --> 00:02:24.930
weeks, predicting
whether they'll

00:02:24.930 --> 00:02:27.870
churn so that you can better
understand your customer.

00:02:27.870 --> 00:02:29.430
The list goes on.

00:02:29.430 --> 00:02:31.710
And these are use
cases that are so core

00:02:31.710 --> 00:02:35.190
to the respective industries
that even small improvements

00:02:35.190 --> 00:02:39.690
in model quality can
have significant business

00:02:39.690 --> 00:02:40.852
implications.

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:46.200
But the challenge
is that especially

00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:50.670
as the number and complexity of
your input columns increases,

00:02:50.670 --> 00:02:53.370
you hit a combinatorial
explosion in things

00:02:53.370 --> 00:02:54.570
you need to worry about.

00:02:54.570 --> 00:02:56.028
So starting from
the left and going

00:02:56.028 --> 00:02:57.930
to the right, on
Data Preparation,

00:02:57.930 --> 00:03:01.020
for every one of your
individual feature columns,

00:03:01.020 --> 00:03:03.390
you need to think about
things like missing values

00:03:03.390 --> 00:03:05.280
and outliers and so forth.

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:07.020
And then under
Feature Engineering,

00:03:07.020 --> 00:03:08.730
for each of those
columns, you need

00:03:08.730 --> 00:03:10.680
to think of the
right pre-processing

00:03:10.680 --> 00:03:12.570
to prepare it for
the following model

00:03:12.570 --> 00:03:15.770
that you select in the
Architecture Selection step.

00:03:15.770 --> 00:03:18.900
And there could be multiple
options per feature column.

00:03:18.900 --> 00:03:21.070
And then under
Architecture Selection,

00:03:21.070 --> 00:03:23.125
there are dozens of models
you could choose from,

00:03:23.125 --> 00:03:25.500
including more that come out
from the research community,

00:03:25.500 --> 00:03:27.855
basically on a monthly basis.

00:03:27.855 --> 00:03:29.230
And then for each
of those models

00:03:29.230 --> 00:03:31.605
you select, you have to select
the right hyperparameters.

00:03:31.605 --> 00:03:34.200
And there could potentially
be a dozen values to set.

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:37.080
And then you have to think about
tuning, think about ensembling,

00:03:37.080 --> 00:03:39.390
if you're trying to create
an especially good model,

00:03:39.390 --> 00:03:41.130
as well as model evaluation.

00:03:41.130 --> 00:03:43.260
And oh, by the way,
if you screw up

00:03:43.260 --> 00:03:45.720
in any one of these
individual steps,

00:03:45.720 --> 00:03:48.030
frequently you
have to start over.

00:03:48.030 --> 00:03:51.370
And this process of iteration
can go on for tens of times,

00:03:51.370 --> 00:03:54.000
especially if you
have a hard data set.

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:55.680
I think data
scientists in the room

00:03:55.680 --> 00:03:58.780
have probably all
experienced that before.

00:03:58.780 --> 00:04:01.840
So this entire process
can take months,

00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:04.920
potentially dooming machine
learning projects altogether,

00:04:04.920 --> 00:04:07.185
as executive sponsors
lose interest.

00:04:10.210 --> 00:04:12.420
So in order to help you
overcome these challenges,

00:04:12.420 --> 00:04:15.060
we decided to build
AutoML Tables,

00:04:15.060 --> 00:04:17.380
a tool for enabling
your entire team,

00:04:17.380 --> 00:04:20.790
whether you're a data scientist,
an analyst, or a developer,

00:04:20.790 --> 00:04:23.550
to automatically build and
deploy state-of-the-art machine

00:04:23.550 --> 00:04:25.950
learning models on
structured data.

00:04:25.950 --> 00:04:28.470
And I'm excited to announce
that AutoML Tables is

00:04:28.470 --> 00:04:31.630
entering into public
beta as of this morning.

00:04:31.630 --> 00:04:33.010
[APPLAUSE]

00:04:35.310 --> 00:04:35.930
Thank you.

00:04:35.930 --> 00:04:37.670
And the way the
product works is we

00:04:37.670 --> 00:04:41.240
provide a graphical, codeless
interface for guiding users

00:04:41.240 --> 00:04:43.530
through the entire end-to-end
machine learning life

00:04:43.530 --> 00:04:46.670
cycle with
significant automation

00:04:46.670 --> 00:04:50.480
as well as guardrails built in
at each individual step, which

00:04:50.480 --> 00:04:53.950
I will show you
shortly inside a demo.

00:04:53.950 --> 00:04:56.680
So we start with helping
you ingest your data

00:04:56.680 --> 00:04:58.330
and then easily define
your data schema

00:04:58.330 --> 00:05:01.780
and target, analyze your
input features in a feature

00:05:01.780 --> 00:05:04.103
statistics dashboard,
automatically train

00:05:04.103 --> 00:05:06.520
your model, including automated
feature engineering, model

00:05:06.520 --> 00:05:08.750
selection, and
hyperparameter tuning,

00:05:08.750 --> 00:05:11.620
as well as evaluate your model
behavior before deploying

00:05:11.620 --> 00:05:14.740
into production, and then
deploying in a single click.

00:05:14.740 --> 00:05:16.990
Through this, we can
help ensure that what

00:05:16.990 --> 00:05:21.010
used to take months of time
only takes weeks or even days.

00:05:23.650 --> 00:05:25.900
Digging deeper into some
of these different steps--

00:05:25.900 --> 00:05:29.320
so on the data ingestion
side, when able,

00:05:29.320 --> 00:05:33.050
we seek to handle data
as found in the wild.

00:05:33.050 --> 00:05:36.183
So we provide automated feature
engineering for, essentially,

00:05:36.183 --> 00:05:37.600
the major data
primitives that you

00:05:37.600 --> 00:05:40.930
can find in BigQuery, things
like numbers, timestamps,

00:05:40.930 --> 00:05:43.630
classes, lists, strings,
and nested fields.

00:05:43.630 --> 00:05:45.790
We do know there are more
other data primitives

00:05:45.790 --> 00:05:47.040
out there that we could cover.

00:05:47.040 --> 00:05:48.920
But this is our starting point.

00:05:48.920 --> 00:05:51.970
And we also worked hard to
ensure that we're resilient to

00:05:51.970 --> 00:05:55.480
and provide guardrails for
imbalanced data, missing

00:05:55.480 --> 00:05:58.030
values, highly correlated
features, high cardinality

00:05:58.030 --> 00:06:00.910
features, like if every
row had a different ID,

00:06:00.910 --> 00:06:01.990
and outliers as well.

00:06:05.000 --> 00:06:06.760
After that, we
automatically searched

00:06:06.760 --> 00:06:08.740
through Google's
entire model zoo

00:06:08.740 --> 00:06:11.740
to find the best model
for you, including

00:06:11.740 --> 00:06:15.340
linear logistic regression for
the smaller, simpler data sets,

00:06:15.340 --> 00:06:19.870
as well as things like deep
neural nets, ensembles, as well

00:06:19.870 --> 00:06:23.080
as architecture search methods,
if you have a larger, more

00:06:23.080 --> 00:06:24.600
complicated data set.

00:06:24.600 --> 00:06:26.440
And one of the benefits
of being at Google

00:06:26.440 --> 00:06:29.020
is we sit next to some of the
best research and consumer

00:06:29.020 --> 00:06:30.490
product teams in the world.

00:06:30.490 --> 00:06:33.280
So we can basically watch
whatever great research results

00:06:33.280 --> 00:06:37.060
come out and take the best and
put into AutoML Tables for you,

00:06:37.060 --> 00:06:39.280
kind of cherry picking
the best for you,

00:06:39.280 --> 00:06:40.660
sometimes even
before results are

00:06:40.660 --> 00:06:44.920
published on them in research
papers, so hot off the press.

00:06:44.920 --> 00:06:47.110
An example of this
is our collaboration

00:06:47.110 --> 00:06:48.760
with the Google Brain team.

00:06:48.760 --> 00:06:53.110
So they delivered what they call
Neural and Tree Architecture

00:06:53.110 --> 00:06:54.040
Search.

00:06:54.040 --> 00:06:55.810
So essentially what
this is is they

00:06:55.810 --> 00:06:58.960
took their architecture search
capability, similar to the one

00:06:58.960 --> 00:07:01.720
that they use for image
classification and translation

00:07:01.720 --> 00:07:02.680
problems.

00:07:02.680 --> 00:07:04.810
And they added
tree-based architectures

00:07:04.810 --> 00:07:07.750
to the existing neural depth
architecture search space,

00:07:07.750 --> 00:07:10.060
and then also added
automated feature engineering

00:07:10.060 --> 00:07:13.270
so that it can work for a wide
variety of structured data.

00:07:13.270 --> 00:07:15.520
Note that this isn't published
in research papers yet,

00:07:15.520 --> 00:07:16.810
so I can't give more details.

00:07:16.810 --> 00:07:18.460
But expect more to
be announced soon.

00:07:21.570 --> 00:07:24.360
And based on the
benchmarks we've done,

00:07:24.360 --> 00:07:26.500
the results speak
for themselves.

00:07:26.500 --> 00:07:29.810
So there are a large number
of vendors in this space.

00:07:29.810 --> 00:07:32.130
And we chose to benchmark
against a subset of them

00:07:32.130 --> 00:07:35.250
with similar functionality.

00:07:35.250 --> 00:07:37.720
And we benchmarked on
Kaggle competitions,

00:07:37.720 --> 00:07:39.720
which I love as a benchmark
because they involve

00:07:39.720 --> 00:07:43.170
real data from real companies
that are putting tens

00:07:43.170 --> 00:07:45.450
to hundreds of thousands
of dollars of prize money

00:07:45.450 --> 00:07:47.640
on the line to find
a good solution.

00:07:47.640 --> 00:07:50.250
And they're willing to wait
months to get a good answer.

00:07:50.250 --> 00:07:52.710
That's typically how long
these competitions take.

00:07:52.710 --> 00:07:55.260
And thousands of data
scientists across the world

00:07:55.260 --> 00:07:56.850
compete in them.

00:07:56.850 --> 00:08:00.450
So if you do well on a
Kaggle competition in terms

00:08:00.450 --> 00:08:02.970
of the ranking, you're
pretty much guaranteed that

00:08:02.970 --> 00:08:05.910
you're doing state-of-the-art
work on a problem that matters

00:08:05.910 --> 00:08:06.960
for the world.

00:08:06.960 --> 00:08:09.450
So that's why we love
this class of benchmarks.

00:08:09.450 --> 00:08:14.490
And here you have,
on the x-axis,

00:08:14.490 --> 00:08:16.920
different Kaggle challenges
that we benchmarked on.

00:08:16.920 --> 00:08:20.670
And on the y-axis is the
percent ranking on, essentially,

00:08:20.670 --> 00:08:25.360
the final Kaggle leaderboard,
if we had actually participated.

00:08:25.360 --> 00:08:27.750
So these challenges
had already ended

00:08:27.750 --> 00:08:29.140
by the time we participated.

00:08:29.140 --> 00:08:31.810
We were just benchmarking
on the final leaderboard

00:08:31.810 --> 00:08:34.080
after the fact.

00:08:34.080 --> 00:08:40.270
And what you'll see is that
most of the time AutoML Tables

00:08:40.270 --> 00:08:43.059
gets into the top
25%, which tends

00:08:43.059 --> 00:08:45.340
to be better than the
existing vendors we tested.

00:08:45.340 --> 00:08:47.530
But caveats apply, of course.

00:08:47.530 --> 00:08:50.197
For some of these data sets, we
were in the middle of the pack.

00:08:50.197 --> 00:08:52.030
And we'll be the first
to admit that we have

00:08:52.030 --> 00:08:53.322
a lot of additional work to do.

00:08:53.322 --> 00:08:56.500
And we're constantly tuning
and improving our systems.

00:08:56.500 --> 00:09:00.890
But when it comes down to it,
in general, we do pretty well.

00:09:00.890 --> 00:09:05.880
So benchmark results are here.

00:09:05.880 --> 00:09:08.858
And I wanted to dig into
one of these benchmarks

00:09:08.858 --> 00:09:10.400
to give you a better
sense of what it

00:09:10.400 --> 00:09:13.280
means to get into the top 25%.

00:09:13.280 --> 00:09:16.280
So here's an example called
the Mercari Price Suggestion

00:09:16.280 --> 00:09:17.170
Challenge.

00:09:17.170 --> 00:09:20.870
Mercari is Japan's biggest
community-powered shopping

00:09:20.870 --> 00:09:22.510
app and marketplace.

00:09:22.510 --> 00:09:24.560
And they created this
challenge for predicting

00:09:24.560 --> 00:09:27.360
the price of a product
offered on their marketplace

00:09:27.360 --> 00:09:30.440
so that they could give pricing
suggestions to their sellers.

00:09:30.440 --> 00:09:33.500
So this is a real version
of the example problem

00:09:33.500 --> 00:09:37.520
that I brought up at the
beginning of this talk.

00:09:37.520 --> 00:09:39.130
So some logistics.

00:09:39.130 --> 00:09:42.280
This challenge went on
for about three months.

00:09:42.280 --> 00:09:45.090
There was a $100,000 prize.

00:09:45.090 --> 00:09:47.590
2,000 data scientists competed.

00:09:47.590 --> 00:09:49.510
And actually, the
winning data scientist

00:09:49.510 --> 00:09:53.980
made about 99 entries in
order to win this competition.

00:09:53.980 --> 00:09:55.840
So it was highly contested.

00:09:55.840 --> 00:09:59.080
It's not just like a pass
through easy challenge, right?

00:09:59.080 --> 00:10:00.580
And the data look
kind of like this.

00:10:00.580 --> 00:10:04.750
So about 1.5 million
rows of offer examples

00:10:04.750 --> 00:10:08.500
like this with some rich
input features, right?

00:10:08.500 --> 00:10:10.420
So you've got the name.

00:10:10.420 --> 00:10:15.430
There are categorical
values, lists of categories,

00:10:15.430 --> 00:10:16.300
item descriptions.

00:10:16.300 --> 00:10:17.677
And some of these are really--

00:10:17.677 --> 00:10:18.760
this is dirty data, right?

00:10:18.760 --> 00:10:20.270
You've got no description yet.

00:10:20.270 --> 00:10:22.480
What is the model
supposed to do with that?

00:10:22.480 --> 00:10:25.847
You've got things like redacted
values over here, right?

00:10:25.847 --> 00:10:27.430
And there were missing
values as well.

00:10:27.430 --> 00:10:29.613
This is a good
real-life data set.

00:10:29.613 --> 00:10:31.780
And the goal is to predict
the price on this column.

00:10:35.300 --> 00:10:36.980
Now, here what
you see is a curve

00:10:36.980 --> 00:10:39.830
where we laid out the
performance of each participant

00:10:39.830 --> 00:10:42.750
in terms of the
final error achieved.

00:10:42.750 --> 00:10:46.200
So the further you
are to the right,

00:10:46.200 --> 00:10:49.160
the better that participant
did on the final leaderboard,

00:10:49.160 --> 00:10:49.980
right?

00:10:49.980 --> 00:10:52.400
And the higher you
are on the y-axis,

00:10:52.400 --> 00:10:55.570
the higher the error
on the final test set.

00:10:55.570 --> 00:10:59.010
So as expected, there is a
line that slopes downwards

00:10:59.010 --> 00:11:01.250
to the bottom right.

00:11:01.250 --> 00:11:04.340
But importantly, it
isn't a consistent curve.

00:11:04.340 --> 00:11:06.430
So what you'll see here
is that, in the beginning,

00:11:06.430 --> 00:11:11.143
there's a steep decline,
right, where better feature

00:11:11.143 --> 00:11:13.310
engineering, better model
selection and all that, it

00:11:13.310 --> 00:11:13.935
really matters.

00:11:13.935 --> 00:11:17.390
There's still more signal to
be squeezed out of the data.

00:11:17.390 --> 00:11:19.160
But then there's a
long plateau where,

00:11:19.160 --> 00:11:20.990
basically, most
of the competitors

00:11:20.990 --> 00:11:24.530
have kind of done whatever they
can to squeeze the signal out

00:11:24.530 --> 00:11:25.920
of the data already.

00:11:25.920 --> 00:11:27.740
So getting to the
top 25% actually

00:11:27.740 --> 00:11:31.020
gets you to that plateau, right?

00:11:31.020 --> 00:11:34.560
And here is how AutoML Tables
does after different numbers

00:11:34.560 --> 00:11:36.310
of hours of training.

00:11:36.310 --> 00:11:39.000
So the way Tables works is you
select the number of hours.

00:11:39.000 --> 00:11:42.890
And here we have Tables
after one hour of training,

00:11:42.890 --> 00:11:46.790
12 hours of training,
and 24 hours of training.

00:11:46.790 --> 00:11:48.710
So caveats, right?

00:11:48.710 --> 00:11:51.290
The Architecture Search, the
hyperparameter tuning process,

00:11:51.290 --> 00:11:53.870
these are all random.

00:11:53.870 --> 00:11:56.720
So if you run this yourself,
it might be a little bit more,

00:11:56.720 --> 00:11:57.770
a little bit less.

00:11:57.770 --> 00:11:59.540
And we did do some
limited data cleaning.

00:11:59.540 --> 00:12:01.550
So for example,
previously, the categories

00:12:01.550 --> 00:12:03.380
were split by slashes.

00:12:03.380 --> 00:12:06.330
So our system just treated
that as one big token.

00:12:06.330 --> 00:12:08.510
So in order to treat
it as individual words,

00:12:08.510 --> 00:12:12.170
you actually have to break out
the slashes by replacing them

00:12:12.170 --> 00:12:13.370
