00:14:23.520 --> 00:14:27.870
as GNP Seguros shortly.

00:14:27.870 --> 00:14:29.730
But before we get
too carried away,

00:14:29.730 --> 00:14:31.620
I'll be the first to
admit that we are not

00:14:31.620 --> 00:14:33.120
perfect for everyone.

00:14:33.120 --> 00:14:35.640
So AutoML Tables is
usually the best fit

00:14:35.640 --> 00:14:37.810
if you meet the
following criteria.

00:14:37.810 --> 00:14:39.870
So first of all,
you have to know how

00:14:39.870 --> 00:14:42.580
to create good training data.

00:14:42.580 --> 00:14:44.820
And this is probably the
most important point, right?

00:14:44.820 --> 00:14:47.070
Somebody in the
room needs to know

00:14:47.070 --> 00:14:50.250
how to take a business
problem and translate it

00:14:50.250 --> 00:14:53.970
into an ML setup, a
classification or regression

00:14:53.970 --> 00:14:55.890
problem of some
sort, right-- binary

00:14:55.890 --> 00:14:58.830
or multi-class classification.

00:14:58.830 --> 00:15:01.900
And that person needs to be
able to take training data,

00:15:01.900 --> 00:15:05.270
especially defining the label
column-- the target column--

00:15:05.270 --> 00:15:07.500
so that it represents
the way you

00:15:07.500 --> 00:15:10.110
want the model to
perform in production.

00:15:10.110 --> 00:15:11.760
And somebody also
needs to understand

00:15:11.760 --> 00:15:15.270
how to make sure that the input
features are the same, in terms

00:15:15.270 --> 00:15:19.653
of the distribution, as what
is available at serving time

00:15:19.653 --> 00:15:21.570
so that you can prevent
training serving skew.

00:15:21.570 --> 00:15:23.430
And a trivial example of this.

00:15:23.430 --> 00:15:25.530
If the training data was
all in English, but then

00:15:25.530 --> 00:15:28.290
at serving time you are
feeding Japanese to the model,

00:15:28.290 --> 00:15:30.770
obviously it would fail.

00:15:30.770 --> 00:15:34.250
Second, you need to be willing
to wait at least an hour.

00:15:34.250 --> 00:15:38.000
So that's our minimum unit
of training, is one hour.

00:15:38.000 --> 00:15:40.730
And if you were looking to
do minute-by-minute iteration

00:15:40.730 --> 00:15:42.870
on your model, potentially
so you can move quickly,

00:15:42.870 --> 00:15:45.800
maybe it's very early in
your development phase,

00:15:45.800 --> 00:15:48.530
then you're probably best
off using something else,

00:15:48.530 --> 00:15:51.830
like a BigQuery machine learning
or Cloud Machine Learning

00:15:51.830 --> 00:15:53.240
Engine.

00:15:53.240 --> 00:15:56.330
And finally, for now--

00:15:56.330 --> 00:15:58.580
I want to call out,
specifically, for now--

00:15:58.580 --> 00:16:02.780
if you have a larger and
more complicated data set,

00:16:02.780 --> 00:16:03.750
that would be ideal.

00:16:03.750 --> 00:16:09.470
So right now, if your data
set is less than 100,000 rows,

00:16:09.470 --> 00:16:12.620
you won't be fully taking
advantage of the advanced

00:16:12.620 --> 00:16:14.090
algorithms that we're using.

00:16:14.090 --> 00:16:15.980
And we're actively
working on this problem.

00:16:15.980 --> 00:16:18.600
And expect more
announcements soon.

00:16:18.600 --> 00:16:21.560
But for now, the bigger
your data set, the better.

00:16:21.560 --> 00:16:24.740
And the flip side of
that is our current limit

00:16:24.740 --> 00:16:27.528
is set at 100 million rows,
just to be conservative.

00:16:27.528 --> 00:16:29.570
But if you have a data
set that goes beyond that,

00:16:29.570 --> 00:16:31.790
actually, we're using Google's
machine learning infrastructure

00:16:31.790 --> 00:16:32.630
under the hood.

00:16:32.630 --> 00:16:35.450
So we're built for
much bigger than that.

00:16:35.450 --> 00:16:37.317
And we would love to
talk to you if you

00:16:37.317 --> 00:16:38.400
have a data set like that.

00:16:38.400 --> 00:16:40.525
So please reach out to your
Google account manager.

00:16:43.370 --> 00:16:46.030
And now I will go over a demo.

00:16:46.030 --> 00:16:46.530
One sec.

00:16:46.530 --> 00:16:48.000
Let me load this
up on my computer.

00:16:53.030 --> 00:16:53.530
Ready.

00:16:53.530 --> 00:16:56.510
Can we do the demo?

00:16:56.510 --> 00:16:57.160
Great.

00:16:57.160 --> 00:17:01.750
So now I'll walk you through the
workflow using AutoML Tables.

00:17:01.750 --> 00:17:05.588
You start by ingesting
that table of data that

00:17:05.588 --> 00:17:06.880
represents your problem, right?

00:17:06.880 --> 00:17:10.579
So it can be from BigQuery
or from Google Cloud Storage.

00:17:10.579 --> 00:17:12.579
So you just select it
from Google Cloud Storage.

00:17:12.579 --> 00:17:14.020
You select your table.

00:17:14.020 --> 00:17:16.160
Say you want to use this one.

00:17:16.160 --> 00:17:17.480
And then you ingest.

00:17:17.480 --> 00:17:20.450
We've already ingested it
to save everybody time.

00:17:20.450 --> 00:17:22.940
So the next step is,
after you ingest,

00:17:22.940 --> 00:17:26.540
you will see a
schema page like this

00:17:26.540 --> 00:17:29.110
where you can fix the
variable type if it's

00:17:29.110 --> 00:17:30.110
set to be the wrong one.

00:17:30.110 --> 00:17:32.270
We do automatic
schema inference.

00:17:32.270 --> 00:17:35.930
But you can update it if it
needs to be updated, right?

00:17:35.930 --> 00:17:38.960
You can also change
the columns in case

00:17:38.960 --> 00:17:41.478
they could potentially
be missing, right?

00:17:41.478 --> 00:17:43.520
So maybe at prediction
time, one of these columns

00:17:43.520 --> 00:17:44.228
might be missing.

00:17:44.228 --> 00:17:47.280
So we can turn on that
it can be nullable.

00:17:47.280 --> 00:17:49.690
And then you select
the target column.

00:17:49.690 --> 00:17:52.800
So for example, in this
problem, actually, the target

00:17:52.800 --> 00:17:55.140
is supposed to be Deposit.

00:17:55.140 --> 00:17:59.010
So this specific data set is
about binary classification,

00:17:59.010 --> 00:18:01.270
predicting, after
marketing outreach,

00:18:01.270 --> 00:18:03.910
whether somebody will make a
deposit at this bank or not.

00:18:03.910 --> 00:18:05.610
So it's a
classification problem.

00:18:05.610 --> 00:18:06.930
And the target is Deposit.

00:18:06.930 --> 00:18:08.472
And if you're an
advanced user, there

00:18:08.472 --> 00:18:10.510
are additional
parameters you can set.

00:18:10.510 --> 00:18:12.660
So for example, you could
set your own data split

00:18:12.660 --> 00:18:16.372
between training evaluation
and holdout sets.

00:18:16.372 --> 00:18:18.330
If certain rows are more
important than others,

00:18:18.330 --> 00:18:20.130
you can set a weight column.

00:18:20.130 --> 00:18:26.600
And if this data has a ordering
over time that matters,

00:18:26.600 --> 00:18:27.980
then you can set a Time column.

00:18:27.980 --> 00:18:30.320
And this will make sure that
the latest data shows up

00:18:30.320 --> 00:18:35.820
in the holdout set instead
of the Training split.

00:18:35.820 --> 00:18:38.440
Next, after you've
set your schema,

00:18:38.440 --> 00:18:42.250
we provide a dashboard of
basic feature statistics

00:18:42.250 --> 00:18:46.340
so that you can detect
common data issues.

00:18:46.340 --> 00:18:52.168
So for example, we allow
filtering by the feature type.

00:18:52.168 --> 00:18:53.710
And there are more
options available.

00:18:53.710 --> 00:18:55.085
But this particular
data set just

00:18:55.085 --> 00:18:58.390
has numeric and categorical
values, to keep it simple.

00:18:58.390 --> 00:19:01.928
And you can potentially see,
OK, what percent of them

00:19:01.928 --> 00:19:02.470
were missing?

00:19:02.470 --> 00:19:05.372
Maybe that could
indicate system problems.

00:19:05.372 --> 00:19:07.330
You can see how many
distinct values there are.

00:19:07.330 --> 00:19:09.340
So for Categorical
Values, especially,

00:19:09.340 --> 00:19:12.700
if you have a cardinality-- the
number of distinct values that

00:19:12.700 --> 00:19:14.142
is similar to the
number of rows--

00:19:14.142 --> 00:19:15.850
then that probably
means that that column

00:19:15.850 --> 00:19:17.680
adds more noise than signal.

00:19:17.680 --> 00:19:19.310
And you should remove it.

00:19:19.310 --> 00:19:21.430
And then finally, you
can detect basic issues,

00:19:21.430 --> 00:19:24.550
like Correlation with
Target for target leakage,

00:19:24.550 --> 00:19:28.300
as well as for
numeric feature types,

00:19:28.300 --> 00:19:30.760
seeing the basic distribution.

00:19:30.760 --> 00:19:36.340
And also, if you deep dive on
any of these particular feature

00:19:36.340 --> 00:19:39.580
columns, you can see things like
the distribution of the values,

00:19:39.580 --> 00:19:43.650
the top correlated features
to it, and so forth.

00:19:43.650 --> 00:19:47.650
We'll keep on adding more
as you ask for them--

00:19:47.650 --> 00:19:50.993
more feature statistic displays.

00:19:50.993 --> 00:19:52.910
Next, after you're
comfortable with your data,

00:19:52.910 --> 00:19:54.320
you can train a model.

00:19:54.320 --> 00:19:57.110
So you set your model name.

00:19:57.110 --> 00:19:59.990
You set how many hours
you want to train for.

00:19:59.990 --> 00:20:02.450
And usually, as you saw
from the Mercari example,

00:20:02.450 --> 00:20:05.160
one hour is a good
place to start.

00:20:05.160 --> 00:20:08.640
You select which feature
columns you want to use.

00:20:08.640 --> 00:20:11.970
We automatically set an
optimization objective for you.

00:20:11.970 --> 00:20:15.195
But if you want to set
an objective yourself,

00:20:15.195 --> 00:20:16.820
because you know that
one of them lines

00:20:16.820 --> 00:20:20.710
up better with your business
goal, you can do that, right?

00:20:20.710 --> 00:20:22.512
And then you train a model.

00:20:22.512 --> 00:20:23.970
I've already trained
a couple here.

00:20:23.970 --> 00:20:25.470
So let's deep dive
into one of them.

00:20:28.900 --> 00:20:31.590
Next is the Evaluation tab.

00:20:31.590 --> 00:20:36.120
So we provide both the overall
performance of that model

00:20:36.120 --> 00:20:38.478
here on the top, as well
some useful metadata,

00:20:38.478 --> 00:20:40.020
like what features
were used and what

00:20:40.020 --> 00:20:43.410
it was set to optimize
for, as well as

00:20:43.410 --> 00:20:45.690
allowing you to export the
predictions on the test set

00:20:45.690 --> 00:20:49.260
to BigQuery if you want to do
additional analysis, as well

00:20:49.260 --> 00:20:54.390
as evaluation metrics on
individual label slices.

00:20:54.390 --> 00:20:58.050
So here, for this data
set, the value one

00:20:58.050 --> 00:21:01.560
means that they did not
make a deposit at the bank.

00:21:01.560 --> 00:21:04.230
And that's 90% of
the data, right?

00:21:04.230 --> 00:21:07.080
So you'll be able to see that
here's the model performance

00:21:07.080 --> 00:21:11.010
for that slice of the data.

00:21:11.010 --> 00:21:14.380
And then two means that
they did make a deposit.

00:21:14.380 --> 00:21:15.800
So that's the minority class.

00:21:15.800 --> 00:21:17.790
And you'll see that it
does slightly worse.

00:21:17.790 --> 00:21:20.790
But that's typically common
for imbalanced data sets.

00:21:20.790 --> 00:21:22.873
And it's up to you to
figure out how you want to--

00:21:22.873 --> 00:21:24.498
whether that's
efficient or whether you

00:21:24.498 --> 00:21:26.340
want to try other
things, like improving

00:21:26.340 --> 00:21:29.320
the weight of the imbalanced
class and so forth.

00:21:29.320 --> 00:21:31.650
So you can flip between
them and kind of

00:21:31.650 --> 00:21:33.080
see the difference, right?

00:21:33.080 --> 00:21:35.640
You can set the score
threshold to see

00:21:35.640 --> 00:21:38.370
how the precision and recall
are at different points

00:21:38.370 --> 00:21:40.510
on that curve.

00:21:40.510 --> 00:21:43.210
If you're interested in
exactly how misclassification

00:21:43.210 --> 00:21:45.250
is happening on
the model, then you

00:21:45.250 --> 00:21:48.140
can look at the Confusion
Matrix to see that.

00:21:48.140 --> 00:21:50.200
And then we have
Feature Importance just

00:21:50.200 --> 00:21:52.600
to give you a sense
of which features

00:21:52.600 --> 00:21:56.640
were the most important for
making those predictions.

00:21:56.640 --> 00:21:59.070
And finally, in
the Predict tab, we

00:21:59.070 --> 00:22:01.710
allow batch prediction
and online prediction.

00:22:01.710 --> 00:22:03.120
So with batch
prediction, you can

00:22:03.120 --> 00:22:06.360
upload a table from BigQuery
or from Google Cloud Storage

00:22:06.360 --> 00:22:08.920
and export your
results there as well.

00:22:08.920 --> 00:22:11.320
And for online prediction,
it's like I said.

00:22:11.320 --> 00:22:12.820
It's one click to
deploy your model.

00:22:12.820 --> 00:22:15.670
And we deploy
globally to make sure

00:22:15.670 --> 00:22:18.380
that you get low
latency serving.

00:22:18.380 --> 00:22:21.830
And then you can test your
model here inside the dashboard.

00:22:21.830 --> 00:22:27.370
So hopefully the demo gods are
kind to me and this is working.

00:22:27.370 --> 00:22:28.250
Great.

00:22:28.250 --> 00:22:30.650
So you can see the
prediction result, right?

00:22:30.650 --> 00:22:33.080
And for regression
problems, we would give you

00:22:33.080 --> 00:22:37.110
a prediction interval, a
95% confidence interval.

00:22:37.110 --> 00:22:39.560
And for classification, we
will provide confidence scores

00:22:39.560 --> 00:22:42.560
for the different labels.

00:22:42.560 --> 00:22:44.720
And that concludes my demo.

