00:24:34.890 --> 00:24:37.880
So there's a lot of
sport to go around.

00:24:37.880 --> 00:24:39.590
The other kind of
contextual thing

00:24:39.590 --> 00:24:43.710
is that Australians have kind of
looked to international sport,

00:24:43.710 --> 00:24:47.227
and they've looked at
football, soccer from Europe.

00:24:47.227 --> 00:24:49.060
And then they've looked
at American football

00:24:49.060 --> 00:24:51.090
and gone, nah, it's not for us.

00:24:51.090 --> 00:24:52.950
We're just going to
invent our own sport.

00:24:52.950 --> 00:24:55.050
So they've invented
Australian rules football.

00:24:55.050 --> 00:24:58.050
It is probably one of the most
popular games in the country.

00:24:58.050 --> 00:25:00.360
Only played in Australia,
professionally.

00:25:00.360 --> 00:25:02.940
But it is a big,
big sport for them.

00:25:02.940 --> 00:25:05.460
So big that in this
little country of about 25

00:25:05.460 --> 00:25:09.570
million people, they pack
out a stadium 90,000 people,

00:25:09.570 --> 00:25:10.900
week in, week out.

00:25:10.900 --> 00:25:12.890
So it's pretty impressive.

00:25:12.890 --> 00:25:16.000
But Australian rules football
is not the only sport played.

00:25:16.000 --> 00:25:18.420
Australia's actually a
pretty divided nation

00:25:18.420 --> 00:25:19.770
when it comes to sport.

00:25:19.770 --> 00:25:22.560
So I'm just kind of highlighting
up some of our key sports

00:25:22.560 --> 00:25:23.200
we have here.

00:25:23.200 --> 00:25:26.700
So we have rugby union at the
top, Australian rules football,

00:25:26.700 --> 00:25:28.740
rugby league, and motor sport.

00:25:28.740 --> 00:25:30.510
Those are the key
broadcast sports

00:25:30.510 --> 00:25:32.080
that we have on Fox Sports.

00:25:32.080 --> 00:25:34.320
But within Australia
itself, certain sports

00:25:34.320 --> 00:25:36.300
have bigger popularity
than the other

00:25:36.300 --> 00:25:39.270
depending on where you live
or where you went to school.

00:25:39.270 --> 00:25:42.480
Rugby league, for example,
really big in Sydney,

00:25:42.480 --> 00:25:43.980
not so much in Melbourne.

00:25:43.980 --> 00:25:46.470
Australian rules football,
really big in Melbourne,

00:25:46.470 --> 00:25:47.850
not so big in Sydney.

00:25:47.850 --> 00:25:49.790
So it's a divided nation.

00:25:49.790 --> 00:25:51.540
So it kind of takes
us through our journey

00:25:51.540 --> 00:25:56.700
at Fox Sports where we have all
these key sports from January

00:25:56.700 --> 00:25:57.870
through to September.

00:25:57.870 --> 00:25:59.060
It's a great lineup.

00:25:59.060 --> 00:26:00.870
There is a bit of a
gap in the summer.

00:26:00.870 --> 00:26:04.500
So last year, we signed
a broadcast rights deal

00:26:04.500 --> 00:26:05.670
for cricket.

00:26:05.670 --> 00:26:07.380
And cricket is the summer sport.

00:26:07.380 --> 00:26:10.590
You will notice that it kind
of looks at October, December.

00:26:10.590 --> 00:26:11.580
We're upside down.

00:26:11.580 --> 00:26:13.408
That is our summertime.

00:26:13.408 --> 00:26:14.700
So that kind of filled the gap.

00:26:14.700 --> 00:26:17.873
And cricket, it costs
us a billion dollars

00:26:17.873 --> 00:26:19.290
for the broadcast
rights for that.

00:26:19.290 --> 00:26:23.950
So there's a little bit of
importance on making it work.

00:26:23.950 --> 00:26:27.960
So we had to look at cricket
from a context of it having

00:26:27.960 --> 00:26:30.197
been on free-to-air television.

00:26:30.197 --> 00:26:32.280
So Australians have been
able to watch it for free

00:26:32.280 --> 00:26:33.330
since day dawn.

00:26:33.330 --> 00:26:35.130
We're now asking
you to pay for it.

00:26:35.130 --> 00:26:38.610
So we had to promise a cricket
experience like never before.

00:26:38.610 --> 00:26:43.060
And this is kind of where
our AutoML Tables came in.

00:26:43.060 --> 00:26:44.790
Now, I'm conscious
of where I'm standing

00:26:44.790 --> 00:26:45.790
and what country I'm in.

00:26:45.790 --> 00:26:47.248
So I just want to
talk a little bit

00:26:47.248 --> 00:26:50.950
about what cricket actually is.

00:26:50.950 --> 00:26:53.590
It's a game that we
sometimes play for five days.

00:26:53.590 --> 00:26:54.790
And no one wins.

00:26:54.790 --> 00:26:56.650
So I kind of think
that blows a few minds.

00:26:56.650 --> 00:26:59.500
In Australia, we
draw quite a lot.

00:26:59.500 --> 00:27:01.570
But it can be like
a chess match.

00:27:01.570 --> 00:27:04.370
So it's really, really tactical.

00:27:04.370 --> 00:27:08.440
And what you'll see is kind
of the last man holding on

00:27:08.440 --> 00:27:12.520
against big Goliath
fast bowlers or the game

00:27:12.520 --> 00:27:15.310
changing on a dime with
three quick wickets.

00:27:15.310 --> 00:27:16.770
It does explode into action.

00:27:16.770 --> 00:27:20.380
But realistically, there are
only 18 seconds of wicket,

00:27:20.380 --> 00:27:23.410
so the wicket being
this guy here.

00:27:23.410 --> 00:27:27.100
The aim of the game is for
this guy to bowl this guy out.

00:27:27.100 --> 00:27:31.180
That really gives us, across
5 days, 18 seconds of action.

00:27:31.180 --> 00:27:32.710
It's a long, long game.

00:27:32.710 --> 00:27:37.330
So what we try and do with
this experiment with the AutoML

00:27:37.330 --> 00:27:40.150
Tables is to warn
fans when we think

00:27:40.150 --> 00:27:42.100
a wicket is going
to happen ahead

00:27:42.100 --> 00:27:44.020
of time so they
know that they need

00:27:44.020 --> 00:27:45.370
to be in front of the screen.

00:27:45.370 --> 00:27:46.640
Don't go to the bathroom.

00:27:46.640 --> 00:27:48.910
Don't go out and do a
barbecue because the action is

00:27:48.910 --> 00:27:50.200
coming up.

00:27:50.200 --> 00:27:54.910
Now, cricket also is a really
great game for structured data.

00:27:54.910 --> 00:27:56.650
There's a lot of
different variables

00:27:56.650 --> 00:27:58.450
that we can measure
to accurately predict

00:27:58.450 --> 00:28:00.560
what we think is going
to happen in the future--

00:28:00.560 --> 00:28:02.013
the types of ball bowled.

00:28:02.013 --> 00:28:02.680
You can do spin.

00:28:02.680 --> 00:28:04.480
You can do fast.

00:28:04.480 --> 00:28:06.590
How long has the batsman
been at the crease?

00:28:06.590 --> 00:28:07.840
Has he been there for an hour?

00:28:07.840 --> 00:28:08.940
Has he been there
for four hours?

00:28:08.940 --> 00:28:10.000
Is he tired, not tired?

00:28:10.000 --> 00:28:11.590
Has he run around a lot?

00:28:11.590 --> 00:28:13.300
Is he the last man standing?

00:28:13.300 --> 00:28:16.570
Generally, the last man
standing is a lesser batsman

00:28:16.570 --> 00:28:18.910
versus the first
guy who goes out.

00:28:18.910 --> 00:28:20.530
What field positions
has the captain

00:28:20.530 --> 00:28:23.100
set out around where they are
looking to catch the ball?

00:28:23.100 --> 00:28:24.560
And about 80 other data points.

00:28:24.560 --> 00:28:26.860
So it makes it an
ideal experiment

00:28:26.860 --> 00:28:29.490
that we tried out last summer.

00:28:29.490 --> 00:28:33.650
And it bloody works, which
was quite impressive.

00:28:33.650 --> 00:28:36.820
So look, I want to let Monty--

00:28:36.820 --> 00:28:38.650
so just a little bit of context.

00:28:38.650 --> 00:28:41.650
We named him Monty because
the first wicket he accurately

00:28:41.650 --> 00:28:44.100
predicted was a chap
called Monty Panesar.

00:28:44.100 --> 00:28:47.240
So it seemed apt to
name him after that.

00:28:47.240 --> 00:28:50.400
So I'm going to introduce you
to-- this is a world first--

00:28:50.400 --> 00:28:54.040
a world's first automated
machine learning commentator.

00:28:54.040 --> 00:28:57.194
I'll let Monty give it
to you in his own words.

00:28:57.194 --> 00:29:00.673
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:29:00.673 --> 00:29:03.655
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:29:10.113 --> 00:29:10.613
- Got it!

00:29:10.613 --> 00:29:12.363
- Why wouldn't you be
happy with that one?

00:29:12.363 --> 00:29:14.589
- Can you believe it?

00:29:14.589 --> 00:29:16.577
- Oh, it's a beauty.

00:29:16.577 --> 00:29:17.571
Yes, yes.

00:29:30.493 --> 00:29:37.948
- [INAUDIBLE] immediately.

00:29:42.421 --> 00:29:43.912
- Oh, what a catch.

00:29:43.912 --> 00:29:45.403
- [INAUDIBLE].

00:29:52.361 --> 00:29:54.846
- Oh, I like that too.

00:29:54.846 --> 00:29:56.834
And Paul Wilson says Yes.

00:30:17.211 --> 00:30:18.702
- Oh, yes.

00:30:18.702 --> 00:30:20.690
Oh, that's such a hit.

00:30:20.690 --> 00:30:21.675
- He's done it again.

00:30:21.675 --> 00:30:23.175
- That is going out
of the ballpark.

00:30:23.175 --> 00:30:24.831
- Absolutely brilliant.

00:30:24.831 --> 00:30:27.648
What an advertisement
for the game.

00:30:27.648 --> 00:30:28.231
[END PLAYBACK]

00:30:28.231 --> 00:30:29.148
JACK SMYTH: All right.

00:30:29.148 --> 00:30:30.388
Thanks so much, Chris.

00:30:30.388 --> 00:30:30.930
Hi, everyone.

00:30:30.930 --> 00:30:31.950
My name's Jack.

00:30:31.950 --> 00:30:33.180
Oh, I'll wait for applause.

00:30:33.180 --> 00:30:34.569
[APPLAUSE]

00:30:37.350 --> 00:30:38.100
My name's Jack.

00:30:38.100 --> 00:30:40.440
And I'll walk you through
how Mindshare created Monty

00:30:40.440 --> 00:30:42.270
in collaboration with Google.

00:30:42.270 --> 00:30:44.880
Now, the first step is to
find a world class data

00:30:44.880 --> 00:30:46.700
provider in Optus Sports.

00:30:46.700 --> 00:30:49.260
So there you track
83 unique variables

00:30:49.260 --> 00:30:50.650
with every single ball.

00:30:50.650 --> 00:30:52.800
And that's available
within seconds of it

00:30:52.800 --> 00:30:54.390
leaving the bowler's hand.

00:30:54.390 --> 00:30:58.050
So we knew we had great training
data and a live feed set up.

00:30:58.050 --> 00:31:00.915
So after that, we could start
experimenting with tables.

00:31:00.915 --> 00:31:02.790
Now, I'll say the first
impressions of tables

00:31:02.790 --> 00:31:03.600
was striking.

00:31:03.600 --> 00:31:07.680
The speed and simplicity of
this platform blew us away.

00:31:07.680 --> 00:31:10.500
We could easily ingest a
year's worth of training data,

00:31:10.500 --> 00:31:12.810
simply select Wickets
as the label to predict.

00:31:12.810 --> 00:31:16.260
And within hours, we were
seeing impressive results.

00:31:16.260 --> 00:31:18.240
To be totally honest,
some of the team members

00:31:18.240 --> 00:31:20.010
even thought that
Monty must be cheating

00:31:20.010 --> 00:31:23.150
because the early results
were simply that good.

00:31:23.150 --> 00:31:25.000
However, those doubts
quickly evaporated

00:31:25.000 --> 00:31:27.130
as we moved through
the training process.

00:31:27.130 --> 00:31:30.310
Tables allowed us to collaborate
a model that could keep pace

00:31:30.310 --> 00:31:31.510
with a live game.

00:31:31.510 --> 00:31:33.610
So we moved ahead with
the classification model

00:31:33.610 --> 00:31:36.040
so we could not only predict
when a wicket would fall

00:31:36.040 --> 00:31:36.835
but how.

00:31:36.835 --> 00:31:39.650
And the final output
looks something like this.

00:31:39.650 --> 00:31:42.340
This is a real example from
a recent game between England

00:31:42.340 --> 00:31:43.410
and the West Indies.

00:31:43.410 --> 00:31:45.160
And you can see the
end point would return

00:31:45.160 --> 00:31:47.050
an analysis of the latest ball.

00:31:47.050 --> 00:31:50.200
And in this case, the batsman's
safe for the next five minutes.

00:31:50.200 --> 00:31:51.510
The prediction is not out.

00:31:51.510 --> 00:31:53.260
But then you can see
the confidence scores

00:31:53.260 --> 00:31:55.330
for each method of
taking a wicket.

00:31:55.330 --> 00:31:58.960
And there's a rising danger
there of him being caught.

00:31:58.960 --> 00:32:01.270
So once we saw the
accuracy during testing,

00:32:01.270 --> 00:32:03.850
we knew that Monty was
ready for the main stage.

00:32:03.850 --> 00:32:05.560
And I have to say,
as Australians,

00:32:05.560 --> 00:32:07.850
we don't really believe
in soft launches.

00:32:07.850 --> 00:32:10.810
So we chose the biggest,
most iconic match

00:32:10.810 --> 00:32:14.320
in the Australian sporting
calendar to debut Monty.

00:32:14.320 --> 00:32:15.860
Now, millions of fans--

00:32:15.860 --> 00:32:16.360
OK.

00:32:16.360 --> 00:32:16.670
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:32:16.670 --> 00:32:17.295
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:32:17.295 --> 00:32:19.170
- The MCG.

00:32:19.170 --> 00:32:22.950
We come here every Boxing
Day for Australia's biggest

00:32:22.950 --> 00:32:24.450
day of cricket.

00:32:24.450 --> 00:32:27.420
Day one of the
Melbourne test match.

00:32:27.420 --> 00:32:31.020
More than 80,000
fans in the stands.

00:32:31.020 --> 00:32:34.560
Millions watching and
listening around the world,

00:32:34.560 --> 00:32:40.730
magically drawn to the
history and the tradition.

00:32:40.730 --> 00:32:43.006
This is where
reputations are made.

00:32:43.006 --> 00:32:43.898
- Got it.

00:32:43.898 --> 00:32:46.130
[INAUDIBLE]

00:32:46.130 --> 00:32:49.280
- This is where Dennis Lillee
knocks over Viv Richards

00:32:49.280 --> 00:32:51.175
with the last bowl of the day.

00:32:51.175 --> 00:32:52.085
- He's got it!

00:32:52.085 --> 00:32:54.820
Oh, what a magnificent
start for Australia.

00:32:54.820 --> 00:32:57.780
- You don't forget that.

00:32:57.780 --> 00:33:01.680
As a kid, you dream of doing
something amazing at the G.

00:33:01.680 --> 00:33:04.434
And a few get lucky.

00:33:04.434 --> 00:33:05.410
- Got him!

00:33:05.410 --> 00:33:06.874
[INAUDIBLE]

00:33:06.874 --> 00:33:07.833
[END PLAYBACK]

00:33:07.833 --> 00:33:08.750
JACK SMYTH: All right.

00:33:08.750 --> 00:33:11.300
So how did it go?

00:33:11.300 --> 00:33:16.080
Monty absolutely-- we
can go to the next slide.

00:33:16.080 --> 00:33:17.540
That was seamless.

00:33:17.540 --> 00:33:19.470
So how did he go?

00:33:19.470 --> 00:33:23.310
Monty absolutely smashed
expectations in his first game.

00:33:23.310 --> 00:33:25.710
We were thrilled
with the accuracy.

00:33:25.710 --> 00:33:28.140
And Tables really came
through when it counted most,

00:33:28.140 --> 00:33:30.960
when we had most of the country
counting on Monty's call.

00:33:30.960 --> 00:33:33.570
So his confidence
scores for each wicket

00:33:33.570 --> 00:33:37.220
were displayed live in ads just
like this for millions of fans.

00:33:37.220 --> 00:33:39.570
And that stretched from push
notifications on the Fox

00:33:39.570 --> 00:33:41.550
Cricket app all the
way through to preroll.

00:33:41.550 --> 00:33:43.830
And we were thrilled with
that accuracy figure.

00:33:43.830 --> 00:33:46.780
That is astonishing
for live sport.

00:33:46.780 --> 00:33:49.170
And I would say as well that
even the moments he missed

00:33:49.170 --> 00:33:50.895
became highlights
in their own right.

00:33:50.895 --> 00:33:52.770
Because that meant that
a bowler had come out

00:33:52.770 --> 00:33:54.990
of nowhere to take a
wicket without warning

00:33:54.990 --> 00:33:57.600
and had literally
beaten the odds.

00:33:57.600 --> 00:33:59.530
Now, as our confidence
grew in Monty,

00:33:59.530 --> 00:34:02.130
we essentially scaled him
up to become the command

00:34:02.130 --> 00:34:04.600
center of our entire campaign.

00:34:04.600 --> 00:34:06.190
So this is the
final architecture.

00:34:06.190 --> 00:34:09.389
And this is largely thanks to an
amazing Googler back in Sydney

00:34:09.389 --> 00:34:10.344
named Drew Jarrett.

00:34:10.344 --> 00:34:12.719
And in the time remaining,
I'll give you a quick overview

00:34:12.719 --> 00:34:14.280
of how it worked.

00:34:14.280 --> 00:34:17.159
Down here, when a ball was
passed through our system

00:34:17.159 --> 00:34:19.903
from Opta, App Engine
would receive that.

00:34:19.903 --> 00:34:21.570
It would pass it
through for processing,

00:34:21.570 --> 00:34:24.360
would use Dataflow to look
at that ball, and also

00:34:24.360 --> 00:34:25.830
an aggregate of
the recent balls,

00:34:25.830 --> 00:34:28.110
to give us what we called
a Prediction Window.

00:34:28.110 --> 00:34:29.760
The model would then
make a prediction

00:34:29.760 --> 00:34:32.250
based on that window
and the individual ball.

00:34:32.250 --> 00:34:34.320
And then App Engine
would facilitate requests

00:34:34.320 --> 00:34:37.320
from live digital billboards,
from the Fox Cricket

00:34:37.320 --> 00:34:41.760
app, Google Ads bidding scripts,
studio dynamic templates,

00:34:41.760 --> 00:34:43.633
and even Google Assistant.

00:34:43.633 --> 00:34:46.050
And I have to say, for me, the
Google Assistant experience

00:34:46.050 --> 00:34:47.580
was a particular highlight.

00:34:47.580 --> 00:34:49.500
This was combining
the predictive power

00:34:49.500 --> 00:34:52.020
of AutoML Tables with
the personalization

00:34:52.020 --> 00:34:53.570
capabilities of Assistant.

00:34:53.570 --> 00:34:55.710
And we could genuinely
offer every fan

00:34:55.710 --> 00:34:58.770
their very own
on-demand commentator.

00:34:58.770 --> 00:35:00.720
So through Assistant,
fans could ask

00:35:00.720 --> 00:35:02.250
for the latest call from Monty.

00:35:02.250 --> 00:35:04.560
They could understand how
he had made that prediction.

00:35:04.560 --> 00:35:06.660
And of course, they get
the latest team news.

00:35:06.660 --> 00:35:08.340
And for me, this is
more than marketing.

00:35:08.340 --> 00:35:11.550
This is an entirely new product
experience made possible

00:35:11.550 --> 00:35:12.560
through Tables.

00:35:12.560 --> 00:35:15.280
And it's one that we
are very, very proud of.

00:35:15.280 --> 00:35:18.790
At the close of the campaign,
the impact of Tables was clear.

00:35:18.790 --> 00:35:22.310
So we saw 150% improvement
in marketing ROI.

00:35:22.310 --> 00:35:25.010
Brand recall for
Fox Sports doubled.

00:35:25.010 --> 00:35:28.550
And the Fox Cricket app
delivered an increase of 140%

00:35:28.550 --> 00:35:30.740
compared to category
competitors.

00:35:30.740 --> 00:35:32.215
So with a scorecard
like that, you

00:35:32.215 --> 00:35:34.340
can be certain you haven't
heard the last of Monty.

00:35:34.340 --> 00:35:35.690
And I'll hand you back to Chris.

00:35:35.690 --> 00:35:36.740
He'll be able to talk
you through where

00:35:36.740 --> 00:35:37.490
we are going next.

00:35:40.670 --> 00:35:41.900
CHRIS POCOCK: Thanks, Jack.

00:35:41.900 --> 00:35:45.380
So those are pretty
damn good results.

00:35:45.380 --> 00:35:49.500
And it, frankly,
took me by surprise

00:35:49.500 --> 00:35:50.870
just how well it all worked.

00:35:50.870 --> 00:35:53.660
So this is not the
end of our journey.

00:35:53.660 --> 00:35:57.200
What we really want to
do is take Monty forward

00:35:57.200 --> 00:36:00.900
and beyond what we've done this
last summer, not only taking it

00:36:00.900 --> 00:36:04.040
into next summer, but
also start applying it

00:36:04.040 --> 00:36:06.710
to some of our winter sports.

00:36:06.710 --> 00:36:09.920
So we're looking at doing
a man versus machine.

00:36:09.920 --> 00:36:12.732
Let Monty take on [INAUDIBLE],,
so panel of experts.

00:36:12.732 --> 00:36:14.690
What do they think the
results are going to be?

00:36:14.690 --> 00:36:17.570
Can they beat AutoML?

00:36:17.570 --> 00:36:20.360
Really excited to see
if they can or can't.

00:36:20.360 --> 00:36:22.760
We also want to be able
to integrate it properly

00:36:22.760 --> 00:36:25.490
on our air, so on
our broadcast, being

00:36:25.490 --> 00:36:28.520
able to allow our
commentators to warn fans when

00:36:28.520 --> 00:36:30.230
exciting moments are upcoming.

00:36:30.230 --> 00:36:31.445
Don't go to the bathroom.

00:36:31.445 --> 00:36:32.570
Make sure you stick around.

00:36:32.570 --> 00:36:34.910
Monty thinks there's
a wicket coming.

00:36:34.910 --> 00:36:39.920
Fantasy is also a fantastic way
of applying Monty's learning

00:36:39.920 --> 00:36:41.090
model.

00:36:41.090 --> 00:36:43.340
Each week, he'll build
his ultimate team.

00:36:43.340 --> 00:36:46.910
And can it beat all of our
consumers, all of our experts,

00:36:46.910 --> 00:36:49.910
week in, week out,
by building based

00:36:49.910 --> 00:36:54.480
on structured data versus
the gut feel of our experts?

00:36:54.480 --> 00:36:55.550
Let's see what happens.

00:36:55.550 --> 00:36:57.330
I'm really excited
for the future.

00:36:57.330 --> 00:36:59.580
It's been an amazing
product, a great journey.

00:36:59.580 --> 00:37:01.010
So thank you to everyone.

00:37:01.010 --> 00:37:03.702
And I appreciate you
listening to our story.

00:37:03.702 --> 00:37:05.030
