TIN-YUN HO: That was awesome.

00:37:07.060 --> 00:37:09.920
CHRIS POCOCK: So now, just
my last role of the day

00:37:09.920 --> 00:37:14.190
is to introduce Enrique and
Carlos from GNP Seguros.

00:37:14.190 --> 00:37:16.220
Hopefully I've
pronounced that properly.

00:37:16.220 --> 00:37:17.660
But welcome, guys,
and thank you.

00:37:20.220 --> 00:37:22.920
ENRIQUE: Thank you very much.

00:37:22.920 --> 00:37:24.750
Well, hello.

00:37:24.750 --> 00:37:26.730
Thank you to our
Tin-Yun and to Google

00:37:26.730 --> 00:37:30.120
for inviting us to share
our experience with AutoML

00:37:30.120 --> 00:37:32.880
at this conference, and
congratulations to Fox

00:37:32.880 --> 00:37:36.740
for such an interesting case.

00:37:36.740 --> 00:37:40.330
GNP is one of the largest
insurance companies in Mexico.

00:37:40.330 --> 00:37:43.870
The company was
founded 116 years ago.

00:37:43.870 --> 00:37:47.130
And we have a yearly sales
of around $3 billion.

00:37:51.860 --> 00:37:55.590
As any large and
well-established company,

00:37:55.590 --> 00:37:59.290
GNP is undergoing a
profound transformation.

00:37:59.290 --> 00:38:01.740
I was going to say
digital transformation,

00:38:01.740 --> 00:38:04.655
but we haven't quite figured
out the precise definition

00:38:04.655 --> 00:38:06.530
of digital transformation,
because everyone's

00:38:06.530 --> 00:38:07.280
talking about it.

00:38:07.280 --> 00:38:09.410
So I'll just mention
transformation.

00:38:09.410 --> 00:38:10.790
But anyway, what
we want to do is

00:38:10.790 --> 00:38:14.660
to modernize our information
systems and our operations.

00:38:14.660 --> 00:38:18.300
And we're leveraging heavily
on the cloud to achieve that.

00:38:18.300 --> 00:38:21.140
And one of the
strategic initiatives

00:38:21.140 --> 00:38:23.780
that we're executing
under this transformation

00:38:23.780 --> 00:38:27.290
is to assemble a single
corporate Data Lake.

00:38:27.290 --> 00:38:30.890
That is a single repository
where all the information

00:38:30.890 --> 00:38:32.720
of the company would reside.

00:38:32.720 --> 00:38:36.200
And besides being an important
enabler of efficiency,

00:38:36.200 --> 00:38:38.990
because we don't have to
figure out where to go and look

00:38:38.990 --> 00:38:40.460
for information
because everything

00:38:40.460 --> 00:38:42.820
is going to be located
or is located already

00:38:42.820 --> 00:38:47.000
in the central Data Lake,
we regard the Data Lake

00:38:47.000 --> 00:38:51.030
as an important source of
competitive differentiation

00:38:51.030 --> 00:38:53.450
and of intelligence.

00:38:53.450 --> 00:38:57.650
And to achieve that, we need
to squeeze knowledge and value

00:38:57.650 --> 00:39:01.250
from all the data that's
stored in the Data Lake.

00:39:01.250 --> 00:39:03.830
And to achieve that,
we have recently

00:39:03.830 --> 00:39:08.870
started to use machine learning
to actually uncover and squeeze

00:39:08.870 --> 00:39:13.050
the value from the information.

00:39:13.050 --> 00:39:15.450
And the problem with
machine learning

00:39:15.450 --> 00:39:21.080
is that the availability of
well-trained data scientists

00:39:21.080 --> 00:39:22.150
is not that great.

00:39:22.150 --> 00:39:26.490
So Carlos is a head of
machine learning at GNP,

00:39:26.490 --> 00:39:28.440
but he's kind of a
rare breed of person,

00:39:28.440 --> 00:39:30.580
because you don't have
that many in the market.

00:39:30.580 --> 00:39:33.360
So when Google offered us the--

00:39:33.360 --> 00:39:36.030
described to us the
concept of AutoML Tables,

00:39:36.030 --> 00:39:38.970
and they offered us the
opportunity to test it,

00:39:38.970 --> 00:39:40.740
we thought it was
absolutely great.

00:39:40.740 --> 00:39:43.650
Because we want to democratize
the use of machine learning

00:39:43.650 --> 00:39:46.260
in the company,
lower the complexity

00:39:46.260 --> 00:39:47.860
of generating new models.

00:39:47.860 --> 00:39:49.920
So it looked very promising.

00:39:49.920 --> 00:39:56.750
And we embarked on testing and
trying this great new tool.

00:39:56.750 --> 00:39:58.970
So basically what
we did is we play

00:39:58.970 --> 00:40:03.230
with a tool with three different
use cases, one in the auto

00:40:03.230 --> 00:40:06.890
industry, in the auto
insurance line of business,

00:40:06.890 --> 00:40:12.120
one in the health
care line business.

00:40:12.120 --> 00:40:18.080
And the third was general
utility for our underwriters

00:40:18.080 --> 00:40:22.080
for collective insurances that
we'll describe a little later.

00:40:22.080 --> 00:40:25.920
So the first example
is really simple.

00:40:25.920 --> 00:40:29.090
It's, given the characteristics
of the insured car

00:40:29.090 --> 00:40:32.180
and of the owner,
try to predict what's

00:40:32.180 --> 00:40:35.030
the probability of that
car of having an accident.

00:40:35.030 --> 00:40:39.080
And to provide the highlights of
this, the technical highlights

00:40:39.080 --> 00:40:44.650
of this exercise, I'll allow
Carlos to share it with you.

00:40:44.650 --> 00:40:48.050
CARLOS: Hello to everyone.

00:40:48.050 --> 00:40:51.340
I will only explain how we're
using AutoML Tables to solve

00:40:51.340 --> 00:40:54.420
insurance problems at GNP.

00:40:54.420 --> 00:40:57.730
As Enrique said, the first
problem is calculating risk.

00:40:57.730 --> 00:41:00.700
This problem consists in
determining and predicting

00:41:00.700 --> 00:41:03.370
the probability of
a car accident using

00:41:03.370 --> 00:41:07.330
drivers' characteristics, such
as age, gender, claim history,

00:41:07.330 --> 00:41:10.390
as well as vehicle
characteristics, such as type,

00:41:10.390 --> 00:41:13.780
model, and intensity usage.

00:41:13.780 --> 00:41:16.180
One thing that is real
amazing about AutoML Tables

00:41:16.180 --> 00:41:18.700
is that you don't have to
worry about future engineering

00:41:18.700 --> 00:41:22.630
or hyperparameter tuning
development issues.

00:41:22.630 --> 00:41:24.430
The only thing
that you need to do

00:41:24.430 --> 00:41:29.180
is the one that Tin-Yu
had in the demo.

00:41:29.180 --> 00:41:31.390
And I want to
highlight that this

00:41:31.390 --> 00:41:34.825
has a very good quality
confirmed by the F

00:41:34.825 --> 00:41:36.850
score in the green square.

00:41:36.850 --> 00:41:40.085
And I will let the next--

00:41:40.085 --> 00:41:46.020
flip to the next problem
Enrique introduced.

00:41:46.020 --> 00:41:48.290
ENRIQUE: So the second
use case that we

00:41:48.290 --> 00:41:51.620
used to try out AutoML Tables
is we had previously already

00:41:51.620 --> 00:41:53.570
developed a machine
learning model

00:41:53.570 --> 00:41:57.930
to try to detect fraudulent
claims in health care.

00:41:57.930 --> 00:42:02.420
And what we did is we created a
new model using AutoML Tables,

00:42:02.420 --> 00:42:04.505
and we compare the
results to their results

00:42:04.505 --> 00:42:07.590
that are already provided by
the existing model that we have.

00:42:07.590 --> 00:42:12.110
And given the characteristics
of the patient, which

00:42:12.110 --> 00:42:14.960
is our customer,
and the sales agent,

00:42:14.960 --> 00:42:16.950
who sold the policy
to this customer,

00:42:16.950 --> 00:42:20.010
and the specific disease
that this person has,

00:42:20.010 --> 00:42:22.610
and the hospital, and
the doctors involved,

00:42:22.610 --> 00:42:26.900
we try to determine if that
claim is fraudulent or not.

00:42:26.900 --> 00:42:30.260
Just to give you an idea, our
yearly expense on health care

00:42:30.260 --> 00:42:33.530
claims, these are
around $680 million.

00:42:33.530 --> 00:42:36.350
So any small
percentage that we can

00:42:36.350 --> 00:42:40.370
achieve in improving
detecting fraudulent claims,

00:42:40.370 --> 00:42:43.190
it translates directly into
a lot of additional revenue

00:42:43.190 --> 00:42:44.600
to the company.

00:42:44.600 --> 00:42:47.090
So I'll let Carlos provide
you with the highlights

00:42:47.090 --> 00:42:49.260
of this exercise.

00:42:49.260 --> 00:42:51.260
CARLOS: OK, thanks, Enrique.

00:42:51.260 --> 00:42:56.000
Again, we use the clients'
claim history, as well as

00:42:56.000 --> 00:42:58.250
medical and hospital
information.

00:42:58.250 --> 00:43:03.230
And one thing that is really
neat about the AutoML Tables,

00:43:03.230 --> 00:43:06.500
is that you have all the
performance evaluations,

00:43:06.500 --> 00:43:09.290
the performance results that
you need at the click distance.

00:43:09.290 --> 00:43:12.290
You don't have to make
additional work in order

00:43:12.290 --> 00:43:14.360
to compute evaluation matrix.

00:43:14.360 --> 00:43:18.110
And I want to highlight
that this model had

00:43:18.110 --> 00:43:23.060
a very high quality compared
by the F1 score of 0.94.

00:43:23.060 --> 00:43:27.490
And we confirmed this quality
on an independent holdout set,

00:43:27.490 --> 00:43:30.090
and I want to highlight that.

00:43:30.090 --> 00:43:33.470
It led to 20% to 30%
improvement in respect

00:43:33.470 --> 00:43:37.740
to our existing ML solutions.

00:43:37.740 --> 00:43:39.990
ENRIQUE: So bottom line,
what we were really impressed

00:43:39.990 --> 00:43:45.360
is, just by using
AutoML, we got a 20%,

00:43:45.360 --> 00:43:49.740
between 20% and 30%
increase in effectiveness

00:43:49.740 --> 00:43:50.730
of detecting claims.

00:43:50.730 --> 00:43:54.900
So that was really promising
and very encouraging to continue

00:43:54.900 --> 00:43:56.610
using this tool.

00:43:56.610 --> 00:43:59.230
And the last example
was a practical example.

00:43:59.230 --> 00:44:01.720
We not only insure individuals.

00:44:01.720 --> 00:44:03.930
We also insure
collectives of people.

00:44:03.930 --> 00:44:06.880
That is, for example, all
the employees of a company.

00:44:06.880 --> 00:44:12.870
So let's say that we're going
to insure all the employees

00:44:12.870 --> 00:44:14.050
of a specific company.

00:44:14.050 --> 00:44:15.870
So what usually
the sales channel

00:44:15.870 --> 00:44:18.060
does is, once that
they do the sale,

00:44:18.060 --> 00:44:21.360
they provide either a
CSV or a spreadsheet

00:44:21.360 --> 00:44:24.000
file to our
underwriting department

00:44:24.000 --> 00:44:26.340
so that they can do the
quote and the underwriting

00:44:26.340 --> 00:44:28.200
of the policy.

00:44:28.200 --> 00:44:30.300
And one of the fields
that is required

00:44:30.300 --> 00:44:32.880
for each of the
persons that integrate

00:44:32.880 --> 00:44:36.750
this collective of people
is the gender of the person.

00:44:36.750 --> 00:44:39.720
But sometimes the
sales channel omits

00:44:39.720 --> 00:44:41.300
to provide that information.

00:44:41.300 --> 00:44:45.090
And when that happens, the
underwriting department,

00:44:45.090 --> 00:44:49.040
what they usually did
before was break up the file

00:44:49.040 --> 00:44:53.200
into many small, different
files and then send that file

00:44:53.200 --> 00:44:54.450
to a lot of different persons.

00:44:54.450 --> 00:44:57.270
And manually each
person will basically

00:44:57.270 --> 00:45:00.290
classify, based on the
name of the person,

00:45:00.290 --> 00:45:02.830
if that person is
male or female.

00:45:02.830 --> 00:45:04.802
So the last time
that that happened,

00:45:04.802 --> 00:45:06.510
one of the last times
that that happened,

00:45:06.510 --> 00:45:08.760
the file was 10,000 rows long.

00:45:08.760 --> 00:45:10.020
So it was a lot of work.

00:45:10.020 --> 00:45:13.170
So one of the persons from
the underwriting department

00:45:13.170 --> 00:45:15.840
approached our data science
team and said, well, you're

00:45:15.840 --> 00:45:17.210
supposed to be smart.

00:45:17.210 --> 00:45:20.890
And there should be a
better way of doing this.

00:45:20.890 --> 00:45:22.710
And it was a simple example.

00:45:22.710 --> 00:45:28.470
What we did is basically we used
our master database as a sort--

00:45:28.470 --> 00:45:31.140
we used the data of
the master database

00:45:31.140 --> 00:45:35.700
to train a model to learn what's
the gender of a person based

00:45:35.700 --> 00:45:39.120
on its name and last
name, its full name.

00:45:39.120 --> 00:45:41.760
So that's basically what we did.

00:45:41.760 --> 00:45:45.610
And that was-- it's a
small problem to solve,

00:45:45.610 --> 00:45:48.510
but it's actually-- it had
a lot of practical usage

00:45:48.510 --> 00:45:52.390
in the company, because this
kind of happens kind of often.

00:45:52.390 --> 00:45:55.260
So it ended up
being a usable tool.

00:45:55.260 --> 00:46:00.190
And I'll let Carlos
describe this exercise.

00:46:00.190 --> 00:46:01.980
CARLOS: Thanks again.

00:46:01.980 --> 00:46:03.570
Well, as you can
see on the screen,

00:46:03.570 --> 00:46:06.660
we made a comparison between
[? A's ?] naive model,

00:46:06.660 --> 00:46:10.000
only using the feature
column of their first name.

00:46:10.000 --> 00:46:16.870
And obviously, it got
a bad quality result.

00:46:16.870 --> 00:46:20.290
And we made a little character
level feature engineering,

00:46:20.290 --> 00:46:23.740
and we recomposed the first
name into suffixes and prefixes.

00:46:23.740 --> 00:46:27.640
And we gain a lot
of performance,

00:46:27.640 --> 00:46:29.080
as it can be seen on the screen.

00:46:29.080 --> 00:46:31.300
This shows the
power on hand that

00:46:31.300 --> 00:46:38.410
makes AutoML Tables when the
relevant variables are chosen.

00:46:38.410 --> 00:46:42.068
Again, I want to thank the
Google Cloud team for letting

00:46:42.068 --> 00:46:43.110
shape the cloud together.

00:46:46.020 --> 00:46:47.828
ENRIQUE: So in this
example, by tweaking

00:46:47.828 --> 00:46:49.370
a little bit the
feature engineering,

00:46:49.370 --> 00:46:55.670
we were able to, on
leveraging on AutoML Tables,

00:46:55.670 --> 00:46:58.380
achieve a very high
efficiency of this model.

00:46:58.380 --> 00:47:00.650
So this is just
another small example

00:47:00.650 --> 00:47:02.840
of how can we use this tool.

00:47:02.840 --> 00:47:07.490
And the results that we saw
in AutoML with the use cases

00:47:07.490 --> 00:47:10.240
that we develop at GNP
are really promising.

00:47:10.240 --> 00:47:13.310
So we're really
excited in the idea

00:47:13.310 --> 00:47:17.090
of leveraging on this
tool to developing

00:47:17.090 --> 00:47:20.240
more machine learning
models much more quickly.

00:47:20.240 --> 00:47:24.410
We are determined to use machine
learning to solve real business

00:47:24.410 --> 00:47:27.260
problems or problems
that are already

00:47:27.260 --> 00:47:28.880
solved with some
sort of automation,

00:47:28.880 --> 00:47:30.530
giving them a much
better solution.

00:47:30.530 --> 00:47:34.100
And one way to achieve
these results quicker

00:47:34.100 --> 00:47:35.975
is through tools like
machine learning.

00:47:35.975 --> 00:47:37.850
And one of the things
that we want to achieve

00:47:37.850 --> 00:47:42.830
is, currently, the underwriting
of medical health care

00:47:42.830 --> 00:47:44.110
goes through a process.

00:47:44.110 --> 00:47:46.970
And using a set of
predefined rules,

00:47:46.970 --> 00:47:52.760
around 55% of all the new
customers, its underwriting

00:47:52.760 --> 00:47:55.290
process is fully automatic.

00:47:55.290 --> 00:47:57.860
And we want to change the
implementation that we have.

00:47:57.860 --> 00:48:01.280
We want to replace the static
rules with a machine learning

00:48:01.280 --> 00:48:05.510
model that would allow
us to have 80% of all

00:48:05.510 --> 00:48:10.280
the underwriting instances to be
fully automatic, and only a 20%

00:48:10.280 --> 00:48:12.590
will be derived to
a set of experts

00:48:12.590 --> 00:48:14.440
to determine the underwriting.

00:48:14.440 --> 00:48:16.790
So that's one of the
targets that we have.

00:48:16.790 --> 00:48:20.750
And on the other end, since
we already compare the results

00:48:20.750 --> 00:48:24.270
that we got with AutoML with the
existing machine learning model

00:48:24.270 --> 00:48:28.970
we have for detecting
fraudulent health care claims,

00:48:28.970 --> 00:48:32.370
we want to improve
in 10% this year

00:48:32.370 --> 00:48:36.410
the amount of fraudulent
claims that we're

00:48:36.410 --> 00:48:39.020
able to detect in
order to contribute

00:48:39.020 --> 00:48:41.450
to additional revenue
in the company.

00:48:41.450 --> 00:48:44.310
So that's basically
what we've done at GNP.

00:48:44.310 --> 00:48:46.280
I think this is a great tool.

00:48:46.280 --> 00:48:47.700
And back to you, Tin-Yu.

00:48:47.700 --> 00:48:48.200
Thanks.

00:48:48.200 --> 00:48:51.250
[APPLAUSE]

